WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.015 --> 00:00:03.160
At this stage,
you might be familiar with the process for

2
00:00:03.160 --> 00:00:05.200
creating tables within a database.

3
00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:09.234
But there are several issues that you're
likely to encounter when working with

4
00:00:09.234 --> 00:00:13.148
tables such as unnecessary data
duplication, issues with updating data and

5
00:00:13.148 --> 00:00:15.690
the effort required to query data.

6
00:00:15.690 --> 00:00:20.760
Fortunately these issues can be resolved
with the use of database normalization.

7
00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:25.224
By the end of this video, you'll be able
to explain what database normalization is

8
00:00:25.224 --> 00:00:29.498
and you'll also be able to demonstrate
an understanding of insert, update and

9
00:00:29.498 --> 00:00:30.733
deletion anomalies.

10
00:00:30.733 --> 00:00:33.943
And be able to list some of
the issues associated with them.

11
00:00:33.943 --> 00:00:38.760
Normalization is an important
process used in database systems.

12
00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:42.952
It structures tables in a way that
minimizes challenges by reducing data

13
00:00:42.952 --> 00:00:46.527
duplication, avoiding data
modification implications and

14
00:00:46.527 --> 00:00:49.634
helping to simplify data
queries from the database.

15
00:00:49.634 --> 00:00:53.793
To gain a better understanding of
normalization and the challenges it

16
00:00:53.793 --> 00:00:58.593
addresses, let's explore an example of
a table that hasn't been normalized.

17
00:00:58.593 --> 00:01:02.327
In this example I'll use
a college Enrollment Table.

18
00:01:02.327 --> 00:01:07.338
The table serves multiple purposes by
providing a list of the college students,

19
00:01:07.338 --> 00:01:09.041
courses and departments.

20
00:01:09.041 --> 00:01:13.126
And the outline of relationships or
associations between students, courses and

21
00:01:13.126 --> 00:01:13.903
departments.

22
00:01:13.903 --> 00:01:17.296
And name and contact details for
the head of each department.

23
00:01:17.296 --> 00:01:21.014
Creating tables that serve multiple
purposes causes serious challenges and

24
00:01:21.014 --> 00:01:23.270
problems for database systems.

25
00:01:23.270 --> 00:01:27.281
The most common of these
challenges include insert anomaly,

26
00:01:27.281 --> 00:01:29.835
update anomaly and deletion anomaly.

27
00:01:29.835 --> 00:01:32.591
Let's begin with an overview
of insert anomaly.

28
00:01:32.591 --> 00:01:37.178
Insert anomaly occurs when new data is
inserted into a table which then requires

29
00:01:37.178 --> 00:01:39.940
the insertion of additional data.

30
00:01:39.940 --> 00:01:43.294
I'll use the college Enrollment Table
to demonstrate an example.

31
00:01:43.294 --> 00:01:45.250
In the college enrollment table,

32
00:01:45.250 --> 00:01:48.660
the student ID column
serves as the primary key.

33
00:01:48.660 --> 00:01:52.893
Each field in a primary key column must
contain data before new records can be

34
00:01:52.893 --> 00:01:55.088
added to any other column on the table.

35
00:01:55.088 --> 00:01:58.803
For example, I can enter a new
course name in the table but

36
00:01:58.803 --> 00:02:02.532
I can't add any new records
until I enroll new students.

37
00:02:02.532 --> 00:02:06.940
And I can't enroll new students
without assigning each student and ID.

38
00:02:06.940 --> 00:02:09.768
The ID column can't contain empty fields.

39
00:02:09.768 --> 00:02:13.456
So, I can't insert a new course
unless I insert new student data.

40
00:02:13.456 --> 00:02:16.161
I've encountered the insert
anomaly problem.

41
00:02:17.190 --> 00:02:21.210
An update anomaly occurs when you
attempt to update a record in a table

42
00:02:21.210 --> 00:02:25.800
column only to discover that this result
in further updates across the table.

43
00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:29.061
Let's return to
the College Enrollment Table, once again,

44
00:02:29.061 --> 00:02:31.454
to understand how
an update anomaly occurs.

45
00:02:31.454 --> 00:02:35.680
In the Enrollment Table, the course and
department information is repeated or

46
00:02:35.680 --> 00:02:38.088
duplicated for
each student on that course.

47
00:02:38.088 --> 00:02:40.735
This duplication increases
database storage and

48
00:02:40.735 --> 00:02:44.350
makes it more difficult
to maintain data changes.

49
00:02:44.350 --> 00:02:47.984
I'll demonstrate this with a scenario
in which Dr. Jones, the Director of

50
00:02:47.984 --> 00:02:52.980
the Computing Department, leaves his post
and is replaced with another director.

51
00:02:52.980 --> 00:02:55.104
I now need to update all instances of Dr.

52
00:02:55.104 --> 00:02:57.737
Jones in the table with
the new Director's name.

53
00:02:57.737 --> 00:03:02.670
And I also need to update the records of
every student enrolled in the department.

54
00:03:02.670 --> 00:03:05.702
This poses a major challenge
because if I miss any students,

55
00:03:05.702 --> 00:03:10.040
then the table will contain inaccurate or
inconsistent information.

56
00:03:10.040 --> 00:03:13.280
This is a prime example of
the update anomaly problem.

57
00:03:13.280 --> 00:03:17.920
Updating data in one column requires
updates in multiple others.

58
00:03:17.920 --> 00:03:20.250
Next let's review the final challenge.

59
00:03:20.250 --> 00:03:22.080
Deletion anomaly.

60
00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:26.311
A deletion anomaly is when the deletion
of a record of data causes the deletion

61
00:03:26.311 --> 00:03:29.540
of more than one set of data
required in the database.

62
00:03:29.540 --> 00:03:30.755
For example, Rose,

63
00:03:30.755 --> 00:03:34.682
the student has signed the ID of four
has decided to leave her course.

64
00:03:34.682 --> 00:03:38.537
So I need to delete her data but
deleting Rose's data results in the loss

65
00:03:38.537 --> 00:03:43.640
of the records for the design department
as they're dependent on Rose and her ID.

66
00:03:43.640 --> 00:03:46.422
This is an example of
the deletion anomaly problem.

67
00:03:46.422 --> 00:03:51.280
Removing one instance of a record of data
causes the deletion of other records.

68
00:03:51.280 --> 00:03:53.680
So, how can you solve these problems?

69
00:03:53.680 --> 00:03:57.640
As you learned earlier,
the answer lies in database normalization.

70
00:03:57.640 --> 00:04:02.161
Normalization optimizes the database
design by creating a single purpose for

71
00:04:02.161 --> 00:04:03.690
each table.

72
00:04:03.690 --> 00:04:07.584
To normalize the College Enrollment Table,
I need to redesign it.

73
00:04:07.584 --> 00:04:08.987
As you discovered earlier,

74
00:04:08.987 --> 00:04:12.830
the table's current design
serves three different purposes.

75
00:04:12.830 --> 00:04:16.243
So the solution is to split
the table in three, essentially,

76
00:04:16.243 --> 00:04:18.617
creating a single table for each purpose.

77
00:04:18.617 --> 00:04:22.680
This means that I now have a Student Table
with information on each student.

78
00:04:22.680 --> 00:04:25.839
A Course Table that contains
the records for each course.

79
00:04:25.839 --> 00:04:29.860
And a Department Table with
information for each department.

80
00:04:29.860 --> 00:04:33.540
This separation of information helps
to fix the anomaly challenges.

81
00:04:33.540 --> 00:04:37.878
It also makes it easier to write sequel
queries in order to search for, sort and

82
00:04:37.878 --> 00:04:39.330
analyze data.

83
00:04:39.330 --> 00:04:42.831
You should now be able to explain
what database normalization is.

84
00:04:42.831 --> 00:04:46.626
And you should also be able to demonstrate
an understanding of anomalies and

85
00:04:46.626 --> 00:04:47.941
challenges, well done.